The files under src/main/resources/RegionData/ are derived from that project's
region data (format version 0.8). Redistribution of those files retains the
copyright notice above as the BSD 2-Clause License requires.

They are no longer byte-identical to upstream, in six ways.

  1. The six citizens in 14131.json were named after the Barrows Brothers, with
     matching examine text, and are now generic "Barrow wight"s; their
     placements, models and animations are unchanged, only the identities.

  2. 12598.json (the Grand Exchange) gained six records that are not theirs at
     all: the "friend cameo" figures, authored for this project and switched off
     by default. They are identifiable in the data by "cameo": true, and they
     are the only records using the "npcAppearanceId" field, which is also this
     project's addition to the format. Nothing upstream authored was touched.

  3. Fourteen vendored citizens had their "moveAnimation" corrected, because it
     named a different creature's skeleton from their "idleAnimation" and their
     models, and a figure cannot have two rigs. Only that one field changed on
     each; placements, models, examine text and remarks are untouched.

       Grimefang, Sludgenose (12594)  HumanWalk        -> GoblinWalk
       Skipper (12596)                HumanWalk        -> PenguinWalk
       Bees! (12853)                  HumanWalk        -> BeeIdle
       Nightfire (12853)              HumanWalk        -> KittenWalk
       Dofur, Simon, Rifur,
         Dorgud (12853), Draug
         (14936)                      HumanWalk        -> DwarfWalk
       Zack (12850)                   DogWalk          -> HumanWalk
       Forester (11061)               DogWalk          -> HumanWalk
       Thalindra, Dark wizard
         (12850)                      DrunkenDwarfWalk -> HumanWalk

     How visible the defect was: thirteen of the fourteen carry no wander box and
     never move, so in all but one case ("Bees!" in Varrock) the wrong gait was
     latent rather than on screen. It was still worth fixing — a latent wrong is
     one wander box away from a visible one — but this notice previously said a
     wrong gait "does not look slightly off, it comes apart", and for thirteen of
     these fourteen nobody could have seen it either way.

     How each replacement was chosen, stated precisely because "we gave each one
     its own species' walk" is only true of nine of them:

       - Nine got the exact walk of the NPC whose model list the record copies:
         Skipper, Bees!, Nightfire, Dofur, Simon, Rifur, Dorgud, Draug and Zack.
       - Grimefang and Sludgenose got GoblinWalk (6202,
         SLICE_SURFACE_GOBLIN_SQUAT_GENERALS_WALK). NPC 3028 "Goblin", which owns
         their exact models, walks on 6180 (SLICE_SURFACE_GOBLIN_SQUAT_WALK)
         instead. Both are goblin framemap 1415, so the skeleton is right and the
         choice is between two goblin walks, but it is not that NPC's own.
       - Forester, Thalindra and the Dark wizard got HumanWalk as a judgement: no
         NPC in the cache owns their exact model lists, so there is no "own walk"
         to take. Every NPC sharing any of Forester's models (1715 of them) and
         any of the Dark wizard's (1870) is on framemap 0. Thalindra is 671 of
         672 — the exception is NPC 14824 "Barb", which shares only model 353, a
         part used by 597 other NPCs.

  4. Four vendored citizens had their "idleAnimation" changed, and this one is
     editorial rather than a defect: they rendered exactly as authored, and what
     they depicted was a person sprawled or distressed in a public street — one
     of them lying flat in Draynor market beside child NPCs. This plugin's brief
     is ambient city life, so each was given an upright pose that keeps the
     character. Examine text and remarks are unchanged in all four; nothing else
     about the records moved.

       Drunken peasant (12597)  HalfLayingDown -> DrunkPlayerReady
       Joe the tramp (12853)    CurledUp       -> VarrockTrampReady
       Pryce (12853)            Crying         -> SlapHead
       Damien (12342)           FallenManIdle  -> NervousIdle

  5. The scenery record at 3169,3488 in 12598.json gained a second model id,
     3818, alongside the 2260 it already had. Every other placement of that
     brazier in their own data — two in 12594, one in 12595, one in 12853 — is
     the pair [2260, 3818]; the Grand Exchange copy was one model short and
     rendered with an open, untextured face.

  6. Three more vendored records had an animation changed, for the reason in
     modification 3 but caught by a different check: the animation drove a
     skeleton the record's own models are not rigged to. Their two child records
     played human emotes on gnome-rigged child models, and a scenery record
     played a human standing animation on a model the game only ever rigs to
     framemap 1944.

       Child (12598)      ChildIdle (EMOTE_LAUGH_LOOP, framemap 0)
                            -> GnomeChildReady (GNOME_READY, framemap 2402)
                          ChildWalk (EMOTE_YES_LOOP, framemap 0)
                            -> GnomeChildWalk (GNOME_WALK, framemap 2402)
       Liam (12852)       ChildIdle -> GnomeChildReady
       Scenery at
         3247,3383 (12852) HumanIdle (HUMAN_READY, framemap 0) -> none

     The two replacements are the "stand" and "walk" the game itself gives the
     NPCs whose model lists those records copy exactly: NPC 3539 "Street urchin"
     for Child, NPC 3306 "Henja" and NPC 3994 "Boy" for Liam. The scenery record
     is model 41886, which is NPC 10570 "Inactive spirit pool" — it renders as
     the static prop it was scaled and translated to be, which is what most
     scenery in this dataset already does. Nothing else on any of the three
     records moved.

BSD-2 permits all six modifications — this note exists so nobody assumes the
vendored data still matches the source it came from.

The animation name -> id table in
src/main/java/com/matthewmariner/livelycities/LivelyAnimation.java is likewise
derived from that project (its AnimationID enum), because the region data
addresses animations by those exact names — the names are part of the data
format, not an implementation choice. Nine constants in it are not theirs, each
added because their table had no name for something a record here needed:

  HumanLeanReady    916    AnimationID.HUMAN_LEAN_READY
  ArmsCrossedReady  2256   AnimationID.RD_KNIGHT_CROSSED_ARMS
  MageReady         5823   AnimationID.TOL_MAGE_READY01
  HumanSmugIdle     14000  AnimationID.HUMAN_SMUG_IDLE
  DrunkPlayerReady  2770   AnimationID.DRUNK_PLAYER_READY
  VarrockTrampReady 6480   AnimationID.VARROCK_TRAMP_READY
  NervousIdle       10680  AnimationID.NERVOUS_IDLE
  GnomeChildReady   2331   AnimationID.GNOME_READY
  GnomeChildWalk    12042  AnimationID.GNOME_WALK

The first four are standing poses for the cameo records above; the next three are
standing poses for the four re-posed citizens in modification 4; the last two are
the small-humanoid rig's own stand and walk, for modification 6. Their table's
"ChildIdle" and "ChildWalk" are still there and still mean what they meant — two
human emotes — they are simply no longer used by anything.

The model lighting parameters in LivelyEntity (ambient 64, contrast 850,
x -30, y -50, z -30, passed to Model#light) are also taken from that project.
Unlike the vertex scale/translate convention around them — which follows
RuneLite's own 128-units-per-tile geometry and is not theirs to own — those five
values are a look, chosen by its authors, and reproducing a look is derivation.

Apart from the three items above, the Java source in this repository is an
independent implementation and is not derived from theirs.

